{
 "type": "FeatureCollection",
 "metadata": {
  "note": "Belfast and Moosehead Lake Railroad, Belfast - Burnham Junction ME, 33.07 mi, no branches. One alignment drawn as two features with identical geometry (Rule 4 split at 1 Jan 1926): Maine Central lease era 1871-1925 in the MEC colour, independent B&ML era 1926-1960 in the B&ML's own colour. Research: research/branches/belfast_and_moosehead_lake_railroad.txt.",
  "not_yet_sourced": [
   "Maine Central lease start: 23 Dec 1870 (Wikipedia, MEC ran it from opening) vs 1871 (american-rails, 'a year later'). Drawn from 1871.",
   "Headway and speed in both eras are estimates; the MEC era has no sourced frequency at all.",
   "Tourist era (c.1988 or early 1990s - 2007; Belfast end ceased 2004 or 2005) is not drawn: its per-year extents (Belfast-side vs Unity-Burnham trips) are not established, and the 1988 vs early-1990s start is in conflict. It would be mode tourist_rail in the B&ML colour.",
   "Heritage operations from 2009 (Brooks Preservation Society / B&ML 2009: Brooks-Waldo, Upper Bridge-Waldo, City Point-Waldo, Unity-based trips) are not drawn; separate entity and extents unsourced here.",
   "Freight-only years 10 Mar 1960 - c.1988/1990 deliberately not drawn (Rule 1).",
   "Belfast terminal: the last ~0.3 mi from the south end of the Belfast Rail Trail to the waterfront depot/yard is not drawn; the depot's exact position was not established.",
   "Which leg of the Burnham Junction wye passenger trains used is not sourced; the north leg is drawn."
  ]
 },
 "features": [
  {
   "type": "Feature",
   "properties": {
    "company": "Maine Central Railroad",
    "line": "Maine Central: Belfast Branch",
    "id": "mec-belfast-branch",
    "color": "#457B9D",
    "avg_speed_kmh": 30,
    "speed_source": "ESTIMATE: no MEC-era timetable was read for running time; set equal to the ~30 km/h measured from the Sept 1951 Official Guide table of the later B&ML era (33.07 mi in ~1h40-1h55).",
    "mode": "intercity_rail",
    "geometry_source": "OpenStreetMap via Overpass (opstrace.py, 2026-10-08)",
    "geometry_quality": "Real-traced from OpenStreetMap (Overpass, mail.ru mirror) with opstrace.py: 32.1 mi on ways named 'Belfast and Moosehead Lake Railroad' / 'Belfast Rail Trail' (old_railway_operator=MEC; railway=rail, disused, or abandoned and now the Belfast Rail Trail path City Point - Belfast), 0.9 mi on OSM rail ways in the same corridor that carry no old_railway_operator tag. No straight-line legs. Traced length 32.76 mi (after 8 m Douglas-Peucker) against the documented 33.07 mi (Sept 1951 Official Guide, Burnham Junction 0 - Belfast 33.07), i.e. 0.31 mi short: the trace starts at the north leg of the Burnham wye where it meets today's Maine Central/CMQ main line (44.6954, -69.4278) and ends at the south end of the Belfast Rail Trail on the Passagassawakeag waterfront just north of the Armistice (Upper/old Route 1) footbridge (44.4311, -69.0102). The last ~0.3 mi to the Belfast depot/yard along the downtown waterfront is lifted, is not in OSM or OpenHistoricalMap, and is NOT drawn rather than guessed. Station sampling (trace milepost vs Guide milepost from Burnham): Winnecook 3.38/3.63, Unity station 8.11/8.12, Thorndike station 11.49/11.67, Brooks 20.77/20.80, Waldo village 25.56/25.92, City Point 30.91/30.91; all within 0.25 km of the line except Knox, whose station position is a hand pin (Nominatim returned Knox County) and lies 1.4 km off - the pin, not the trace, is the uncertain one. Alignment: the line was never relocated in a way known to this research; the traced track is the surviving 1870 right-of-way, so no later-alignment substitution is known. The Burnham Junction end uses the north (Bangor-facing) wye leg; which leg passenger trains used is not sourced.",
    "segments": [
     {
      "start": 1871,
      "end": 1925,
      "headway_min": 480,
      "note": "Belfast and Moosehead Lake Railroad (chartered 1867) opened 23 Dec 1870 and was run from the start by the Maine Central as its Belfast Branch, under lease, until the MEC gave up the lease on 1 Jan 1926 (Wikipedia; american-rails agrees on the 1926 end). LEASE START CONFLICT, recorded not resolved: Wikipedia has MEC running the line from opening day, 23 Dec 1870; american-rails says it was leased 'a year later' (1871). Drawn from 1871, collapsing inward past the one disputed week of 1870. branchline.uk's Maine file (section 35) lists the principal stations in MEC public timetables of 1892/94, 1911 and 1921, confirming passenger service through the era. Stations: Burnham Jct, Winnecook, Unity, Thorndike, (Fobes, flag, 1913), Knox, Brooks, Waldo, (Sargents, flag, 1921), City Point, Belfast. headway_min is an ESTIMATE (about two round trips a day assumed); no MEC-era frequency was sourced.",
      "sources": [
       "Segment-level: this segment's own note names the sources for its dates (Wikipedia and american-rails for the 1870/71 start and 1 Jan 1926 end; branchline.uk for timetable presence), as established by the research report research/branches/belfast_and_moosehead_lake_railroad.txt. The headway is not sourced.",
       "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belfast_and_Moosehead_Lake_Railroad_(1871%E2%80%932007)",
       "https://www.american-rails.com/moosehead.html",
       "https://www.branchline.uk/jfpdf/mainerrs.pdf"
      ]
     }
    ]
   },
